al with flush and straight possibilities. It's below average for starting hands in heads-up play.\n\n**Opponent's Action**: They raised to 300 (3x the big blind), which is a standard opening raise. This doesn't tell us much - they could have a wide range of hands in heads-up play.\n\n**Promising Moves**:\n\n1. **Fold (-1)**: Conservative play. Saves 200 chips (we're already in for 100 as big blind). Against a raise, J-6 suited is vulnerable even though it's suited. This minimizes loss on a weak hand.\n\n2. **Call (0)**: Costs 200 more chips to see the flop. J-6 suited has some playability - we could hit a flush draw, straight draw, or favorable board. Pot odds are reasonable (we'd be getting about 2.5:1 on our call). In heads-up play, calling ranges are wider than in full ring games.\n\n3. **Raise to 600+**: Aggressive play trying to take initiative or fold out opponent. However, with J-6 suited, we don't have enough equity to turn this into a profitable bluff-raise, and we're out of position post-flop.\n\n4. **All-in (-2)**: Way too aggressive for J-6 suited. This would be a massive overbet and we'd likely only get called by better hands.\n\n**Position**: We're in the big blind, so we'll be out of position post-flop, which is a disadvantage.\n\nReason:\nWhile J-6 suited has some potential, it's a marginal hand that performs poorly against a raising range. In heads-up play, I should play somewhat looser than in full ring games, but calling 200 chips with a hand that's difficult to play post-flop (especially out of position) isn't optimal. The suited connector gives us some potential, but we're more likely to miss the flop and face continued aggression. Since this is only hand 2 of 20, preserving chips and waiting for better spots is prudent. The -100 chip deficit is small and easily recoverable with better hands.\n\nAnswer: -1"}
